    "post_rendered": "For a fixed natural number <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/6/5f6fa6546da3adb7c8422b7d96a499c43757390c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\geq 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" >,</span> prove that<br>\n<br>\n<b>a.)</b> There exists integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/d/15dc718b6f91dc51e0b27ca3f8582e98793604e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{2m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"112\" height=\"11\" > such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/3/3a3db85d505700abe8263781da2ca96bb658000b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x_i x_{m + i} = x_{i + 1} x_{m + i - 1} + 1, i = 1, 2, \\ldots, m \\hspace{2cm}(*)\\]\"  width=\"433\" height=\"18\" ><br>\n<br>\n<b>b.)</b> For any set of integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/b/62bfa97e9a76bc2ab749f946d5e87fe2d3c85a64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lbrace x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{2m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"122\" height=\"18\" > which fulfils (*), an integral sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/f/b0fbd8e3acd8d419c7027cff0f03f241b637e18f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ldots, y_{-k}, \\ldots, y_{-1}, y_0, y_1, \\ldots, y_k, \\ldots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"261\" height=\"11\" > can be constructed such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/4/294260f0cb2758a7ed46204e9ba8f70e1be12116.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y_k y_{m + k} = y_{k + 1} y_{m + k - 1} + 1, k = 0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\ldots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"343\" height=\"16\" > such that <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/b/cfb9cf4ca540cfce867005ca4b15cbae1c9fce3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y_i = x_i, i = 1, 2, \\ldots, 2m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"184\" height=\"16\" >.</span>",
    "post_canonical": "For a fixed natural number $m \\geq 2$, prove that\r\n\r\n[b]a.)[/b] There exists integers $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{2m}$ such that \\[x_i x_{m + i} = x_{i + 1} x_{m + i - 1} + 1, i = 1, 2, \\ldots, m \\hspace{2cm}(*)\\]\r\n\r\n[b]b.)[/b] For any set of integers $\\lbrace x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{2m}$ which fulfils (*), an integral sequence $\\ldots, y_{-k}, \\ldots, y_{-1}, y_0, y_1, \\ldots, y_k, \\ldots$ can be constructed such that $y_k y_{m + k} = y_{k + 1} y_{m + k - 1} + 1, k = 0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\ldots$ such that $y_i = x_i, i = 1, 2, \\ldots, 2m$.",
