    "post_rendered": "For every permutation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/7/5670c41d2fd17fe243083eac9a55bc9a320837ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ \\tau$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"8\" > of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/8/e5886fb7ce84ef9006ce32751f7cd8fd8f0a221c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ 1, 2, \\ldots, 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"84\" height=\"16\" >,</span> <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/2/d42d1bd28d3331bd2b038f5fd11642f2220e8fe9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ \\tau = (x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{10})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"155\" height=\"18\" >,</span> define <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/a/00a5f244c238c8bae1455b5641d5f50b53bad85f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ S(\\tau) = \\sum_{k = 1}^{10} |2x_k - 3x_{k - 1}|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"192\" height=\"50\" >.</span>  Let <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/0/ec0ed9d4adad339f2683fad6b88dcc71476875c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ x_{11} = x_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"64\" height=\"10\" >.</span>  Find<br>\n<br>\n<b>I.</b> The maximum and minimum values of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/1/8e1d14a7d792301e3e9adf54e01c6bb3b716aea7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ S(\\tau)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\n<b>II.</b> The number of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/7/5670c41d2fd17fe243083eac9a55bc9a320837ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ \\tau$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"8\" > which lets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/1/8e1d14a7d792301e3e9adf54e01c6bb3b716aea7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ S(\\tau)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" > attain its maximum.<br>\n<br>\n<b>III.</b> The number of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/7/5670c41d2fd17fe243083eac9a55bc9a320837ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ \\tau$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"8\" > which lets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/1/8e1d14a7d792301e3e9adf54e01c6bb3b716aea7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ S(\\tau)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" > attain its minimum.",
    "post_canonical": "For every permutation $ \\tau$ of $ 1, 2, \\ldots, 10$, $ \\tau \\equal{} (x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{10})$, define $ S(\\tau) \\equal{} \\sum_{k \\equal{} 1}^{10} |2x_k \\minus{} 3x_{k \\minus{} 1}|$.  Let $ x_{11} \\equal{} x_1$.  Find\r\n\r\n[b]I.[/b] The maximum and minimum values of $ S(\\tau)$.\r\n\r\n[b]II.[/b] The number of $ \\tau$ which lets $ S(\\tau)$ attain its maximum.\r\n\r\n[b]III.[/b] The number of $ \\tau$ which lets $ S(\\tau)$ attain its minimum.",
